You also asked — may God, glorified be He, grant you success in all that pleases Him — about the prayer of greeting the mosque: is it obligatory only within the mosque itself, never in its courtyard and forecourt? And if someone enters the mosque and performs it there, then goes out to the courtyard and returns, is he obliged to perform it again on account of that departure, or not?

Here too, with the granting of success from God, glorified be He, you have reached the sound word on the matter. The truth of it is this: the greeting prayer is obligatory only within the mosque itself — not in its courtyard and not in its forecourt. If it is performed in those areas, that is good, since they have become part of the mosque and within its bounds. Therefore, if a person inside the mosque goes out to the courtyard and returns — as I have explained — he is not obliged to repeat it, because he has not yet gone out from the mosque's bounds.

The book *Tawil al-Sharia* records, relating it from the Prophet (ع), that he said:

> لا تقام الحدود في المساجد، ومن سمعتموه ينشد ضالته في مسجد فقولوا: لا وجدت

The prescribed penalties are not to be carried out in the mosques; and whoever you hear calling out for his lost property in a mosque, say to him: May you not find it.

The same book records that the Prophet (ع) said:

> لا بأس بالصلاة في مرابض الغنم

There is no harm in praying in the folds of sheep.

The author of *al-Majalis al-Mustansiriyya* — may the peace of God be upon its author — said in the eighth assembly: Hear now what we set before you concerning its meaning: prayer has seven ranks, by which the prayers of those who pray are graded one above another:

1. the prayer of the worshipper in his house, as a single prayer;
2. the prayer of the worshipper in the mosque of the market, as twelve prayers;
3. the prayer of the worshipper in the mosque of the tribe, as twenty-five prayers;
4. the prayer of the worshipper in the congregational mosque, as one hundred prayers;
5. the prayer of the worshipper in the Farthest Mosque, as one thousand prayers;
6. the prayer of the worshipper in the mosque of the Messenger of God, as ten thousand prayers;
7. the prayer of the worshipper in the Sacred House of God, as one hundred thousand prayers.

For every one of these there are abundant meanings and likenesses of wisdom.

Our lord, the Commander of the Believers, Ali ibn Abi Talib (ع), said in his four hundred rules of conduct:

fluency-19-part-01:35fac7ac8592aee17bfab.part:
I turn now to the mention of supplication after the prayer.

In the question-and-answer book arranged according to the order of the chapters of Pillars of Islam, the question was put: what do you say concerning what is found in the books of the guiding call, that supplication is not recited after the afternoon prayer? Is what is meant the supplication of drawing near, or all supplications? The answer given was: drawing near and all else — and if anyone does supplicate, no sin rests upon him.

A further question was asked: does the worshipper supplicate after the obligatory afternoon prayer or not, and does he wipe his face at the place of prostration at the close of every prayer or not? The answer: it is not permitted except after the afternoon prayer and after dawn. Our masters, may God sanctify their souls, commanded wiping after the afternoon prayer and after dawn in particular, and this is among what they received from the pure Imams (عليهم السلام). It has been mentioned that one does not prolong supplication after the afternoon prayer, yet if he does prolong it, nothing rests upon him.

The book of the interpretation of the Sharia states that if the prayer falls in the daytime the recitation is kept low; if it falls at night, in the dominion of darkness, the voice is raised; and the dawn prayer, offered in the last darkness of the night, is announced aloud.

Our lord and master, the author of the Mustansiriyya Councils, peace of God be upon him, said in the Eleventh Council: having brought the discussion of the jurisprudence of prayer to the limit set out above — where the outward receives its exposition and its expoundings, and the inward its sign and its intimation — we follow it with what is now read to you: the mention of the supplication that comes at the close of the prayer, and what it contains of the seven and the twelve, according to the manner in which our explanation has proceeded throughout, for the benefit of those of understanding who take it to heart.

Of the seven, these are they:

1. the supplication of asking (دعاء المسألة), in which the supplicant spreads out his hands and sets the inner surface of his palms facing his face;
2. the supplication of seeking refuge (دعاء الاستعاذة) from what he fears, in which he turns his palms about and sets their backs facing his face;
3. the supplication of humble submission (دعاء الاستكانة), in which he grasps his shoulders with his hands — this is done only in solitude;
4. the supplication of devotion (دعاء التبتل), in which he points with the index finger;
5. the supplication of entreaty (دعاء التضرع), which is made by moving the finger;
6. the supplication of fervent appeal (دعاء الابتهال), in which he spreads his palms and extends both hands together, raised toward the sky;
7. the supplication of abasement in the dust (دعاء التعفير), in which he supplicates while turning his cheeks upon the ground.

For each of these seven supplications there is a mention in the Quran, which we shall bring forward when we return to the explanation of what we have read, for everything we have set down is of the nature of roots — like the roots of a tree awaiting the branching of its boughs and the bringing forth of its fruit.

As for the twelve, they are the foundations of supplication and the base of its building. Whoever desires that his supplication be answered should do as follows:

1. send ahead of it repentance from what God has forbidden;
2. or a charity that he gives;
3. or a fast;
4. or a prayer — for God, mighty and majestic, says:

> إليه يصعد الكلم الطيب والعمل الصالح يرفعه

To Him ascends the good word, and the righteous deed raises it;

5. then purify his intention in the supplication and turn toward God with his whole heart;
6. begin with the praise of God, exalted is He, and with extolling Him as He deserves;
7. call down blessings upon the Prophet at the beginning of the supplication, in its middle, and at its end;
8. draw near to God, exalted is He, through the imams of the religion, naming them one by one until he reaches the imam of his own age;
9. seek access through him and through them to God in the answering of his supplication;
10. then ask for the good of this world and the hereafter;
11. seek refuge from the evil of this world and the hereafter;
12. then supplicate after that for whatever he loves and wills.

Our master, the Commander of the Faithful, Ali ibn Abi Talib (ع), said in his Four Hundred Etiquettes:

fluency-19-part-08:7995966757d258f236667.part:
> ولا ترفع صوتك فوق صوته

Do not raise your voice above his voice — meaning the voice of the imam.

It also says that the recitation in the noon prayer and in the afternoon prayer is not made aloud but is kept low, because these belong to the prayer of the daytime. And it says that in the night prayer the recitation is made aloud. And it says that the Prophet forbade wiping over the leather socks and forbade omitting the audible utterance of the words "In the name of God, the Merciful, the Compassionate."

> المصلي إذا كبر لا يتكلم حتى يسلم

Once the worshipper has pronounced the opening magnification, he does not speak until he gives the salutation.

Our master Jafar ibn Mansur al-Yaman, may God exalt his sanctity, said in his book on the inward nursing, concerning the meaning of prayer: the prayer has doors that are numbered, and their full number must be completed, for by them the prayer is made complete. They are:

1. the ablution;
2. the opening;
3. the call to prayer;
4. the standing call;
5. the turning toward God;
6. the recitation;
7. the bowing;
8. the praise;
9. the glorification;
10. the prostration;
11. the testimony;
12. the salutation;
13. the supplication of the standing invocation;
14. the supplication of the odd-numbered prayer.

These are fourteen doors from which the worshipper has no escape. They are the supporting pillars of the prayer, and without them his prayer is not complete.

